\section{Trim a Recursive Continuation Interface}

Counting parameter uses naively keeps a parameter that is only passed
back to itself. A local continuation group permits a stronger test:
start from parameters read by real computations, then follow the
connections induced by tail-call arguments backward. For this version
assume targets are known, there are no escaping continuations, and
arguments are already evaluated variables or literals. This last
restriction matters: removing an argument expression must not remove
a trap or effect. Computations, branch conditions, and return operands
are left untouched.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Local continuations and indexed tail-call arguments
\Let{L}{\operatorname{parametersReadOutsideCallArguments}()}
\Let{W}{\operatorname{queue}(L)}
\While{$W$ is not empty}
  \Let{p}{\operatorname{pop}(W)}
  \To{A demanded formal makes its supplying argument matter.}
  \ForAll{parameters $q$ supplied to $p$ by a call}
    \If{$q\notin L$}
      \State Add $q$ to $L$ and $W$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndWhile
\To{Project both ends of every call edge by the same mask.}
\State Remove formals outside $L$ and their actual argument slots
\end{sudocode}

Extend the summing loop's header to \verb|head(i, s, tag)| and its
entry call to \verb|head(0, 0, 99)|. The backedge calls
\verb|head(next_i, next_s, tag)|, and nothing else reads \texttt{tag}.
The algorithm seeds $i$ and $s$ from the arithmetic, test, and return,
but never demands the tag slot. Removing that slot from both calls
and from the signature leaves the result $3$ at $n=3$. For a
negative test, return the tag on exit instead; its slot is now
demanded and must be kept, so the result is $99$.

At convergence every retained formal receives a corresponding argument
on every incoming call, and no retained expression reads a deleted
binding. Simultaneous binding still holds because both lists are
projected in the same order. With an index from formals to incoming
argument slots, each demanded position and its slots are visited once.
The test is conservative when ordinary instructions compute values
used only as arguments to deleted slots: their operands initially
count as real reads. A subsequent dead-code pass can remove those
instructions if doing so is legal, after which interface trimming may
find additional dead parameters.

\section{Choose a Notation That Exposes Rewrite Obligations}

When a join is written as a parameterized label, some graph rewrites become easier to state. A parameter with no uses can be removed together with its argument at every call site. An argument passed unchanged by all incoming edges can instead be captured when its definition dominates the label; otherwise the apparent agreement is not enough to introduce the free reference. Block argument lists also make edge redirection an interface change: the replacement edge must supply the right types and arity, including any effect state. A round-trip test should compare branch behavior and observable effects under renaming, not demand that the starting graph be recovered verbatim: inlining \texttt{body} and \texttt{exit} in the example already changed that graph.
